 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (the print's runs of dots after Ah!, piacer and l'amor!). Musetta sings offstage, from the tavern; her \"sta il\" is sung on one note. The chorus (the street sweepers at the gate, the women and men singing in the tavern, the milkmaids, the carters and the peasant women) sings in the score and is heard with the piano; only the soloists' lines are given here: Musetta and the customs officer. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced. Translation literal, for meaning.",
